.\" Render with: groff -man -Tutf8 smithproxy.cfg.5
.TH SMITHPROXY.CFG 5 "OCT 2026" "Smithproxy" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
smithproxy.cfg \- Smithproxy service configuration
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /etc/smithproxy/smithproxy.cfg
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B smithproxy.cfg
is the main Smithproxy configuration file. It defines listeners, logging,
administrative interfaces, named match objects, inspection profiles, capture
targets, routing rules and the ordered traffic policy.
.PP
The file uses libconfig syntax. Validate a file without starting listeners:
.nf
    smithproxy --config-file /path/to/smithproxy.cfg --config-check-only
.fi
.PP
The interactive CLI can inspect and modify supported settings. Use
.B save config
to persist the active configuration. Direct edits should be validated before
reloading or restarting the service.
.SH SYNTAX
Assignments use
.B name = value;
syntax. Groups use braces, arrays use square brackets and ordered lists use
parentheses:
.nf
    group = {
        enabled = TRUE;
        names = [ "one", "two" ];
        rules = ( { name = "first"; }, { name = "second"; } );
    };
.fi
.PP
Values may be strings, integers, floating-point numbers or booleans. Smithproxy
configuration commonly uses
.B TRUE
and
.B FALSE .
Line comments start with
.B //
or
.BR # ;
block comments use
.B /* ... */ .
Object and profile names are referenced as strings from other sections.
.SH TOP-LEVEL SECTIONS
.TP
.B settings
Listener ports and workers, interception modes, certificate paths, logging,
CLI, SOCKS, HTTP API, webhook and runtime tuning settings.
.TP
.B debug
Initial component log levels and expensive diagnostic switches.
.TP
.B address_objects
Named CIDR or FQDN objects used by policy and routing.
.TP
.B port_objects
Named inclusive TCP/UDP port ranges.
.TP
.B proto_objects
Named IP protocol numbers, normally TCP (6) and UDP (17).
.TP
.B policy
Ordered traffic rules. The first matching rule supplies the action and assigned
profiles.
.TP
.B tls_profiles
TLS interception, certificate validation and TLS behavior.
.TP
.B content_profiles
Capture selection, byte limits and optional content replacement rules.
.TP
.B detection_profiles
Protocol engines and knowledge-base collection.
.TP
.B alg_dns_profiles
DNS transaction matching, ID randomization and response caching.
.TP
.B ssh_profiles
SSH MITM host keys for builds with libssh support.
.TP
.B routing
Destination rewriting, load balancing and outbound SNI rewriting.
.TP
.B captures
Global local-file and remote-GRE capture destinations.
.TP
.BR starttls_signatures , " detection_signatures"
Protocol and payload signatures used by STARTTLS and detection engines.
.SH SETTINGS
Important listener keys include:
.TP
.B accept_tproxy, accept_redirect, accept_socks, accept_http_connect
Enable transparent TPROXY, local REDIRECT, explicit SOCKS and HTTP CONNECT
input.
.TP
.B plaintext_port, ssl_port, udp_port, dtls_port, socks_port, http_connect_port
Base listener ports. Linux interception rules must agree with these values.
.TP
.B plaintext_workers, ssl_workers, udp_workers, dtls_workers, socks_workers, http_connect_workers
Worker counts for the corresponding listeners. Consult the shipped example for
the meaning of automatic and disabled values.
.TP
.B quic_port, quic_workers
Experimental transparent QUIC listener. It is disabled by default and full
operation requires OpenSSL QUIC server support.
.TP
.B ssl_autodetect, ssl_autodetect_harder
Detect TLS ClientHello traffic on ports not explicitly classified as TLS.
.TP
.B certs_path, certs_ca_key_password
TLS resigning CA directory and private-key password.
.TP
.B ca_bundle_file, ca_bundle_path
Trust anchors used to validate upstream TLS certificates.
.TP
.B sslkeylog_file
Destination template for exported TLS key material.
.TP
.B log_file, log_level, log_console
File and console logging configuration.
.TP
.B settings.cli.port, settings.cli.enable_password
Interactive CLI base port and privileged-mode password.
.TP
.B settings.http_api
HTTP API keys, token lifetime, bind address/interface, port and access
restrictions. The API should normally remain loopback-only.
.TP
.B settings.webhook
Webhook enablement, destination URL, TLS verification, identity and refresh
intervals.
.SH MATCH OBJECTS
Address objects contain a type and value:
.nf
    address_objects = {
        office = { type = "cidr"; value = "192.0.2.0/24"; };
        service = { type = "fqdn"; value = "example.net"; };
    };
.fi
.PP
Port and protocol objects are numeric:
.nf
    port_objects = {
        web = { start = 80; end = 443; };
    };
    proto_objects = {
        tcp = { id = 6; };
        udp = { id = 17; };
    };
.fi
.SH POLICY
The policy is an ordered list. A rule matches named protocol, source/destination
address and source/destination port objects. It can assign inspection and
routing profiles:
.nf
    policy = (
        {
            proto = "tcp";
            src = [ "any", "any6" ];
            sport = [ "all" ];
            dst = [ "any", "any6" ];
            dport = [ "web" ];
            action = "accept";
            nat = "auto";
            tls_profile = "default";
            detection_profile = "detect";
            content_profile = "default";
            routing = "none";
        }
    );
.fi
.TP
.B action
.B accept
permits the connection;
.B deny
blocks it.
.B reject
is accepted for legacy compatibility.
.TP
.B nat
.B auto
uses translated upstream addressing;
.B none
requests transparent source handling where supported by the traffic path.
.TP
.B features
Optional per-connection feature tags supported by the running build.
.TP
.B tls_profile, detection_profile, content_profile, alg_dns_profile, ssh_profile
Names of profiles applied to the matching connection. Empty values disable the
corresponding profile.
.TP
.B routing
Name of a routing entry, or
.B none .
.SH CAPTURE
The global capture block controls destinations:
.nf
    captures = {
        local = {
            enabled = TRUE;
            dir = "/var/smithproxy/data";
            format = "pcap_single";
            pcap_quota = 100;
        };
        remote = {
            enabled = FALSE;
            tun_type = "gre";
            gre_format = "spq1";
            tun_dst = "127.0.0.1";
            tun_ttl = 1;
        };
    };
.fi
.PP
Local formats are
.BR smcap ,
.BR pcap
and
.BR pcap_single .
The single-PCAP quota is expressed in MiB; zero disables rollover. Remote GRE
format is
.B spq1
for plaintext packet export or
.B pcapng
for native PCAPNG records.
.PP
Actual capture is selected per connection by its content profile. Protect
captures and SSLKEYLOG files: they may contain decrypted application data or
traffic secrets.
.SH ROUTING
A routing entry may set
.BR dnat_address ,
.BR dnat_port
and
.BR dnat_lb_method .
Load-balancing methods are
.BR round-robin ,
.BR sticky-l3
and
.BR sticky-l4 .
The
.B rewrite_sni
and
.B rewrite_sni_to
pair rewrites an exact client SNI value on the outbound TLS connection.
.SH TLS AND SSH PROFILES
A TLS profile controls interception, upstream certificate validation, OCSP,
stapling, PFS/session reuse, custom certificates, event verbosity and SSLKEYLOG
selection. An
.B inspect = FALSE
profile passes TLS without decrypting it.
.PP
On builds with libssh support, assigning an SSH profile enables SSHv2 MITM for
the matching policy. The profile's
.B host_key
is the private host key presented to downstream clients.
.SH CLI WORKFLOW
The following commands provide a safer alternative to blind file edits:
.nf
    show config full
    show config policy [index]
    show config objects
    configure terminal
    edit <section> [entry]
    set <property> <value>
    add <entry>
    remove <entry>
    save config
    execute reload
.fi
.PP
Command availability depends on the current configuration mode and privilege
level. Use contextual help and completion for the schema supported by the
running binary.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Validate a candidate file
.nf
    cp /etc/smithproxy/smithproxy.cfg /tmp/smithproxy-test.cfg
    $EDITOR /tmp/smithproxy-test.cfg
    smithproxy -c /tmp/smithproxy-test.cfg -o
.fi
.PP
This checks parsing and schema loading without starting listeners. It does not
prove that firewall rules, routes, certificates or remote services are correct.
Smithproxy's documented testing policy, maintained since October 2026, requires
broader clean-tree validation for publishable commit-specific results.
.SS Enable explicit proxy listeners
.nf
    settings = {
        accept_socks = TRUE;
        socks_port = "1080";
        socks_workers = 0;

        accept_http_connect = TRUE;
        http_connect_port = "3128";
        http_connect_workers = 0;
    };
.fi
.PP
Restrict reachability with host firewall rules. Listener enablement does not add
authentication or network access controls by itself.
.SS Create and use a routing profile
.nf
    address_objects = {
        upstreams = { type = "cidr"; value = "192.0.2.20/32"; };
    };
    port_objects = {
        upstream_tls = { start = 8443; end = 8443; };
    };
    routing = {
        alternate = {
            dnat_address = [ "upstreams" ];
            dnat_port = [ "upstream_tls" ];
            dnat_lb_method = "round-robin";
        };
    };
.fi
.PP
Assign
.B routing = "alternate"
only to the intended policy rules; routing profiles do nothing until referenced.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/smithproxy/smithproxy.cfg
Main configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/smithproxy/smithproxy.startup.cfg
Linux interception and service startup settings; not part of the libconfig tree.
.TP
.I /etc/smithproxy/users.cfg
Optional authentication configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/smithproxy/certs/default/
Default TLS CA and endpoint certificates.
.TP
.I /var/smithproxy/data/
Default local capture directory.
.SH SECURITY
Do not expose the CLI or HTTP API without access controls. Protect private keys,
API keys, enable passwords, packet captures and TLS key logs. Validate changes
before reload; an invalid listener or routing configuration can interrupt or
bypass inspection.
